---
title: "Start/Stop Cell Editing"
---

Customize when and how editing starts and stops using configuration options, keyboard shortcuts, or the Grid API.

Once you've [enabled editing](./cell-editing/) on your columns, the grid provides several ways to customize editing behavior beyond the defaults.

## Customizing Start Triggers

### Single-Click Editing

By default, double-click starts editing. Set `singleClickEdit = true` on the grid or column to start editing with a single click.

```{% frameworkTransform=true %}
const gridOptions = {
    // Enable single-click editing for all columns
    singleClickEdit: true,
};
```

You can also configure single-click editing per column:

```{% frameworkTransform=true %}
const gridOptions = {
    columnDefs: [
        { field: 'country', singleClickEdit: true },
        // Other columns use double-click
    ],
};
```

{% gridExampleRunner title="Single Click Editing" name="single-click-editing"  exampleHeight=520 /%}

### No-Click Editing

Prevent clicks from starting editing by setting `suppressClickEdit = true`. This is useful when you want to trigger editing programmatically, such as with a button in a custom cell renderer.

```{% frameworkTransform=true %}
const gridOptions = {
    suppressClickEdit: true,
};
```

{% gridExampleRunner title="No Click Editing" name="single-click-editing-renderer"  exampleHeight=520 /%}

## Customizing Stop Behavior

Editing stops when focus moves to another cell, or when specific keys are pressed ({% kbd "↵ Enter" /%} to accept, {% kbd "⎋ Esc" /%} to cancel, {% kbd "⇥ Tab" /%} to move to next cell). You can also stop editing programmatically using the Grid API.

### Stop When Grid Loses Focus

By default, the grid continues editing when the cell loses focus unless another grid cell is clicked. To stop editing when focus leaves the grid entirely, set `stopEditingWhenCellsLoseFocus = true`:

```{% frameworkTransform=true %}
const gridOptions = {
    stopEditingWhenCellsLoseFocus: true,
};
```

The example below demonstrates this behaviour:

-   Double-click to start editing 'Age', then click outside the grid (on the 'Dummy Save' button or text field) and editing stops
-   Double-click to start editing 'Year' (custom popup editor), click anywhere on the popup, but clicking outside closes it

{% gridExampleRunner title="Stop Editing When Grid Loses Focus" name="stop-edit-when-grid-loses-focus"  exampleHeight=510 /%}

## Navigation During Editing

### Tab Navigation

While editing, press {% kbd "⇥ Tab" /%} to stop editing the current cell and start editing the next cell. Press {% kbd "⇧ Shift" /%}+{% kbd "⇥ Tab" /%} to move to the previous cell. This matches Excel behaviour.

Navigate programmatically using the Grid API:

{% apiDocumentation source="grid-api/api.json" section="navigation" names=["tabToNextCell", "tabToPreviousCell"] /%}

Both methods return `true` if navigation was successful, otherwise `false`.

### Enter Key Navigation

By default, pressing {% kbd "↵ Enter" /%} starts or stops editing without moving focus. Configure vertical navigation for Excel-like behaviour:

-   `enterNavigatesVertically`: Set to `true` to have {% kbd "↵ Enter" /%} move focus to the cell below when not editing. Default is {% kbd "↵ Enter" /%} starts editing the focused cell.
-   `enterNavigatesVerticallyAfterEdit`: Set to `true` to have {% kbd "↵ Enter" /%} move focus to the cell below after editing. Default is editing stops and focus remains on the current cell.

```{% frameworkTransform=true %}
const gridOptions = {
    enterNavigatesVertically: true,
    enterNavigatesVerticallyAfterEdit: true,
};
```

{% gridExampleRunner title="Enter Key Navigation" name="enter-key-navigation"  exampleHeight=555 /%}

## Programmatic Control

Control editing programmatically with the Grid API:

{% apiDocumentation source="grid-api/api.json" section="editing" names=["startEditingCell", "stopEditing", "getEditingCells", "getEditRowValues", "isEditing"] /%}

`getEditingCells()` returns an array of objects containing details of currently editing cells. Typically this array contains one entry, but when using [Full Row Edit](./cell-editing-full-row/), multiple cells may be editing simultaneously.

The example below demonstrates the editing API with buttons that:

-   `edit()`: Start editing normally
-   `edit(Backspace)`: Start editing as if {% kbd "Backspace" /%} was pressed (clears contents first)
-   `edit('T')`: Start editing with 'T' as the initial value
-   `edit(top)`: Edit the top pinned row
-   `edit(bottom)`: Edit the bottom pinned row
-   `stop()`: Stop editing
-   `next()`: Navigate to next cell
-   `previous()`: Navigate to previous cell
-   `which()`: Log which cell is currently editing

{% gridExampleRunner title="Cell Editing API" name="cell-editing"  exampleHeight=545 /%}

Below is a code example showing how to use the editing API methods:

```{% frameworkTransform=true %}
// Start editing country cell on first row
api.startEditingCell({
    rowIndex: 0,
    colKey: 'country'
});

// Stop editing
api.stopEditing();

// Print details of editing cells
const cellDefs = api.getEditingCells();
cellDefs.forEach(cellDef => {
    console.log(cellDef.rowIndex);
    console.log(cellDef.column.getId());
    console.log(cellDef.floating);
});
```

## Start/Stop Events

Listen for editing lifecycle events:

{% apiDocumentation source="grid-events/events.json" section="editing" names=["cellEditingStarted", "cellEditingStopped"] /%}

These events fire when editing starts and stops, allowing you to respond to user interactions or trigger custom logic.

